WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.767
-You know our first guest
tonight from his work

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.033
in "Parks and Recreation"

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:05.901
and blockbuster franchises
like "Jurassic World"

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:07.601
and Marvel's
"Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.934
He stars in the
"Super Mario Bros. Movie,"

00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:11.434
which is in theaters
and IMAX now.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:12.667
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:14.567
-Just pop in this pipe,
and we're on our way.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.901
-Oh.
-It's the only way to fly, man.

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.367
[ Warp pipe noise ]
[ Laughing ]

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:20.133
Whoo!

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.501
-Oh, wow.
Love these pipe--

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:22.567
Aaah!!

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.434
Yaaaah!
Unh!

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.234
Ugh!
Unh!

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.534
Ohh!

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:30.868
-Whoo!
-Unh!

00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:32.467
[ Warp pipe noise ]

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:33.701
[ Groans ]

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:36.234
-Here we are -- palace doors.
Bing bang boom.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:39.300
♪♪

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.234
Come on!
Whoo!

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:43.701
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:43.701 --> 00:00:45.934
our good friend
Chris Pratt, everybody!

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:47.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:57.934
♪♪

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:08.000
♪♪

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.901
♪♪

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:15.501
Welcome back, old friend.
-Whoo!

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:17.467
It's good to be here.

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:20.634
-You brought a box.
-I brought a very special box

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:22.667
directly from the
Mushroom Kingdom to you.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:23.968
-Oh, my God.
This is beautiful.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:25.133
Look at this --
says, "Super Mario Bros."

00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.734
-Courtesy of --
-There you go.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.334
-It's the
"Super Mario Bros. Movie."

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:33.033
It's an official collectible.
They're very rare.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.601
-Ooh!
-Coin.

00:01:34.601 --> 00:01:36.467
-Wow! Look at that.
-Yes, yes.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.033
-That's a super...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:41.868
-I bashed my skull against
numerous bricks in the city

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.434
finding that.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.434 --> 00:01:45.267
So I hope you appreciate it.
-I do appreciate it.

00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:46.734
You know what
I bet you appreciate.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.467
Since I last saw you, you have
had two baby girls.

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.300
-Man, I have, yeah.
-Congratulations.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.934
-Between you and I,
we're gonna repopulate.

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:56.968
-We are gonna repop--
I added -- I added a girl

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:58.701
since I last saw you, as well.
-Amazing.

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.300
-Here we go. How is it?
How's being a girl dad?

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.601
-I love it, man. It's really --
It's really great.

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:06.067
Obviously, you know,
I do have a 10-year-old son,

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.400
so this is my first time
having daughters.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.701
And, man, it's just --
It's fantastic.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.701
They really -- you know,
you hear that maybe

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:16.367
the experience is different.
And it really is.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:20.501
And in my experience, I mean, my
my daughter Lyla is so girly.

00:02:20.501 --> 00:02:22.701
She picks out all --
I picked her up yesterday.

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:24.834
She goes, "Oh, daddy,
that's a cute outfit.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:26.267
Look, you have
little cute pockets."

00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:27.634
[ Laughter ]
That's not something

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.534
Jack ever said to me.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:32.200
-Yeah, my boys have
not mentioned my pockets once.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.534
-No, no. Yeah.
-And I put a lot of work

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:35.868
into my pockets.
-Yeah, I know you!

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:37.968
It's nice to have
a little appreciation

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:39.968
for the pocket game.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:41.901
-Now, I am also --

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:44.501
You have a father-in-law...
-Yes.

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:45.868
-...of note.
Your father-in-law

00:02:45.868 --> 00:02:47.501
is Arnold Schwarzenegger.
-Correct. Yes, yes.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:50.701
-Now, that is --
that's got to be surreal,

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:54.300
because I'm assuming we're kind
of from the same generation.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.133
He's a huge deal.
He's an inspiration.

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:57.934
-Yeah, it is pretty surreal.

00:02:57.934 --> 00:02:59.601
I mean, I've gotten
to obviously know him now

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:02.200
as just a regular guy
and all of that.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.334
He's a great grandfather,

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:05.501
a great dad,
a good father-in-law,

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:08.901
and he's got a wealth
of experience, oddly,

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:12.267
in the world of promoting
blockbuster action movies.

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:13.934
It's like, who else are you
gonna talk to

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:16.300
about that kind of stuff?
So that's pretty cool.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.801
But, yeah.
-My father-in-law would never

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:19.033
be able to help me out
with that.

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:20.734
-"Let me ask you something.
What do you think?

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:21.934
What should I say?"

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.467
But it's been --
it's been very nice.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:26.534
He's a great dude.
We play chess a lot.

00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:28.834
And he loves his grandchildren.

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:31.067
-Who's better at chess?
-He -- I've never beat him.

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.334
-Okay.
-He's incredible.

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:33.567
He's really good.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.634
-I've spent a very little
amount of time with him,

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:36.934
but the most fun thing
about spending time

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:38.167
with Arnold Schwarzenegger
is he sounds

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:39.467
exactly like
Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:40.868
-Yeah, right?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.734
Yeah. You're like, "That's a
really good Arnold you do.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:46.567
That's -- Wow, that's good."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:48.968
-Now, was this --
was "Super Mario Bros.",

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:50.701
was that a game
you were playing as a kid?

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:53.200
-Man, I -- yes.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.467
Honestly,
if I had to count up the hours,

00:03:57.467 --> 00:03:58.834
and this is no hyperbole,

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:00.400
I think it's close to
25,000,000 hours...

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.767
...that I played
"Super Mario Bros."

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:06.667
I saw the movie,
and the movie's fantastic,

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:08.400
and there's all these
great Easter eggs,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.000
and the -- and the sound,
the soundtrack,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.067
it lifts from the soundtrack
of the games.

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:15.534
And I just had this Pavlovian
response to like,

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:17.334
the coin and -- and landing
on a turtle

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:18.934
and breaking a brick
and getting the star.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:20.434
I was like, "Br-br-br!"
I just like --

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.100
[ Laughter ]
I've been -- 30 years

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.767
I've been preparing
for this movie.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:26.467
And as an audience,
you really kind of --

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.701
you realize just how hypnotizing
that music has been.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.968
So I don't think people
are quite ready

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:35.634
for what's coming to them,
but it's very promising for us.

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.667
I mean, I think there's going
to be a lot of these movies

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:39.234
coming out
because it is so good.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:42.601
-And, Jack, your son, he also
dabbles in this game, right?

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:44.434
He knows it?
-He's a "Smash Bros" guy.

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:45.634
-Okay, gotcha.
-You know, he plays "Smash"

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.734
on his -- on his Switch.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.434
So he hasn't played --
I don't think we've played

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.634
the original
Nintendo Entertainment System

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:52.767
"Super Mario Bros." game,

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:54.400
the game that I played
so much as a kid.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.367
But he plays like "Mario Kart"
and "Smash Bros."

00:04:56.367 --> 00:04:58.667
-Were you also --
were you the generation

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.200
that would actually go
to the arcade

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.100
and play coin-operated games?
-I was.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.400
I was right in that sweet spot.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:04.868
So, it was late '80s

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:07.501
when I first was introduced
to the "Super Mario Bros."

00:05:07.501 --> 00:05:09.434
or "Mario Bros."
arcade-style game.

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:13.234
I grew up in a very small town,
and there wasn't anything to do.

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.667
And so I'd come home
from school,

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:16.400
and I would walk
across the street

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.367
to this little
coin-operated laundromat,

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:21.501
Fowlds Laundry, still
in Lake Stevens, Washington.

00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:24.434
And there was
an arcade game there.

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:26.901
I never had a quarter, but I
would just stare at the game.

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:28.100
I'd hit the buttons.
I was like,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.634
"Maybe one day
I'll be able to play this."

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:32.434
And then I found out
that Alfy's Pizza,

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:33.667
also still in Lake Stevens,

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:36.033
had a jukebox that was
tucked into the corner,

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.434
and the coin return
was on the back side.

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.000
So people would put
their money in --

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.367
"Eh, this piece of crap
ate my coins."

00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:43.834
But I knew
they were in the back.

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:45.801
And my -- my little
9-year-old arm

00:05:45.801 --> 00:05:49.133
would reach back in,
dislocate my shoulder pulling --

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:50.267
I checked --
That was my routine.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:51.501
Every day after school,

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:53.400
go to Alfy's,
check the coin return.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.067
If there were coins,
go to Fowlds.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.534
-So you were just walking
back and forth?

00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:01.067
-Yeah. I beat a pa--
I beat a triangular path

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:02.467
between Alfy's and Fowlds.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:04.834
-Was there something about
somebody would give you money

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:06.033
if you got in the dryer?

00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:07.601
-Yes. Yeah.

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:11.467
If I -- if coin fishing
didn't work out at Alfy's,

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:13.501
I'd talk to Jason,
who was the teenager

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:15.033
who seemed like an adult
at the time,

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:18.033
but he was the teenager
who operated the --

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:19.367
or, you know, ran the desk.

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.133
-At the laundromat.
-At the laundromat.

00:06:21.133 --> 00:06:22.934
And he would give me a quarter.

00:06:22.934 --> 00:06:25.300
In exchange, I'd have to get
in the tumble dryer.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.434
[ Laughter ]
And he'd hit tumble dry.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:30.701
This is kind of win-win
for me as a kid.

00:06:30.701 --> 00:06:32.501
-I would say!
-It's pretty great.

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:33.767
-As a kid, what are
you complaining about?

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:36.234
-Giant tumble dryer?
I'm like, "Pa-tunk, pa-tunk,

00:06:36.234 --> 00:06:37.701
pa-tunk!"
"I can't believe it!

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:39.400
I'm living the dream!"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.000
And then I get the quarter.
It was amazing.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.634
-I mean, I guess all my
questions for this

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:45.901
are really probably for Jason.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:47.868
-And also we have
some members of --

00:06:47.868 --> 00:06:49.968
some -- some
gentlemen in uniform

00:06:49.968 --> 00:06:52.300
that'd like to talk
to Jason, too, about...

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.167
-Putting a --throwing a kid
in a tumble dryer.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:56.100
-Yeah, I loved it, though.
I mean, I was --

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.400
I always wanted to be a stuntman
growing up,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:58.567
so I was always --
my brother and I

00:06:58.567 --> 00:06:59.801
were always throwing ourselves
down the stairs.

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:01.267
-Yeah, so tumble dryer
was nothing.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:02.334
-Tumble dryer's nothing.
-Yeah, come on.

00:07:02.334 --> 00:07:03.634
-Yeah, it's easy quarter.

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:05.667
-It was the easiest quarter
I ever made.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.267
-Best five minutes of my life
for that quarter.

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:08.834
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.834 --> 00:07:10.734
I come out a little smaller.

00:07:10.734 --> 00:07:12.567
-You know, last week,
we had your old

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:14.767
"Parks and Rec" colleague
Adam Scott on the show.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:16.267
-Yeah!

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:18.367
And, you know, I'm wondering,
do you guys still keep in touch?

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:20.100
I mean, it was a really
close group of people.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.100
You were here in 2015,
I guess, was it

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:23.834
where we all -- we had
a cast show where you were

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.000
all out here
for the season finale.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.400
-Yeah, I remember that.
I think, like --

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.634
Wasn't Aubrey
making out with Jim O'Heir?

00:07:29.634 --> 00:07:31.667
-Yeah, here.
-And Nick was playing guitar.

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:33.534
And -- Oh, those were
the good old days, man.

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:34.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.801 --> 00:07:37.300
Yeah, yeah. We still --
we still keep in touch.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.234
We have a text thread that goes,
and, you know,

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.734
people's birthdays
and celebrations.

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:43.033
And obviously so many
of the cast members

00:07:43.033 --> 00:07:44.133
are doing such amazing things.

00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:45.234
-It's really nice time
for all of you.

00:07:45.234 --> 00:07:46.734
-Checking in
and supporting each other.

00:07:46.734 --> 00:07:49.033
Yeah, for sure. We still have
that text thread going.

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:50.701
-And you're gonna
have to start another one

00:07:50.701 --> 00:07:52.100
because your time with
"Guardians"

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.200
is coming to a close, as well,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.467
which has been
an incredible journey

00:07:54.467 --> 00:07:56.300
with another special
group of people.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.367
-Thanks, guys. Thank you. Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:58.367 --> 00:08:00.701
-I think people forget that,
you know,

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:02.901
"Guardians" wasn't
a property people knew as well.

00:08:02.901 --> 00:08:06.901
And it was -- I don't think
people had the same anticipation

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:08.901
or thought it was gonna do
as well as it ended up doing.

00:08:08.901 --> 00:08:10.501
-No, they really didn't.
I mean, I remember --

00:08:10.501 --> 00:08:13.834
In fact, on our last day of
shooting on "Volume 3,"

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:14.934
we knew this was the end,

00:08:14.934 --> 00:08:16.601
and -- and it was
a little bit like,

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:18.367
you know, the last day
of your senior year.

00:08:18.367 --> 00:08:19.968
It's like
you're excited for summer,

00:08:19.968 --> 00:08:21.634
but are you ever gonna
see your friends again?

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:22.801
It's like that --
that trepidation.

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:24.734
You don't really want
to walk away.

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:26.467
And I was just --
I addressed the cast

00:08:26.467 --> 00:08:27.834
and the crew and the director,

00:08:27.834 --> 00:08:30.100
and I said -- and I
pulled up from my phone

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.334
like about 10 headlines
from the press

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:34.734
when the movie
was being released,

00:08:34.734 --> 00:08:38.434
all of which completely
predicted this massive bomb.

00:08:38.434 --> 00:08:40.901
They're like, "Are you ready?"
I remember the press.

00:08:40.901 --> 00:08:42.701
"Are you ready to be
Marvel's first big bomb?"

00:08:42.701 --> 00:08:44.367
"Why Is anyone gonna
watch this movie?"

00:08:44.367 --> 00:08:46.634
"No one gives a crap about
the Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:08:46.634 --> 00:08:48.167
"Wait. It's a raccoon
and a tree?"

00:08:48.167 --> 00:08:50.033
"There's no A-list stars
in this thing."

00:08:50.033 --> 00:08:52.501
"So this is going to suck."
And I'm like, "Oh, my God."

00:08:52.501 --> 00:08:53.901
At the time, you know,
I was like,

00:08:53.901 --> 00:08:55.601
"It probably is gonna suck,
and this is it for me!

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:58.801
At least I got to try."
And then, you know, it came out,

00:08:58.801 --> 00:09:00.834
and by the grace of God,
it did very well.

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:02.734
And we're still working, so...
-Yeah.

00:09:02.734 --> 00:09:04.501
[ Cheers and applause ]
Well, it's really exciting.

00:09:04.501 --> 00:09:06.167
Man, what a delight
to have you back here.

00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:07.467
-Dude, it's --
-It's really always so special

00:09:07.467 --> 00:09:08.868
to see you.
Thank you so much.

00:09:08.868 --> 00:09:11.467
You guys, that's Chris Pratt.
"Super Mario Bros.",

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:13.300
theaters and IMAX now.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:14.801
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

